Honour the caller's requested load size in the PNG and EXR plugins.

FreeImage already lets a caller pass a maximum dimension in the high bits of
the load flags, and PluginJPEG uses it to drive libjpeg's DCT scaling. Nothing
else does, so for every other format a caller that wants a smaller image has to
decode at full size first and rescale afterwards -- which needs exactly the peak
allocation it was trying to avoid. An 8K RGBA EXR needs around a gigabyte before
anything can shrink it.

Extend the same convention to PNG and EXR by subsampling during the read, so
only one source row is ever held besides the (smaller) destination.

PNG: libpng has no scaled read, so png_read_row every row and keep every Nth.
Skipped for interlaced images, which cannot be read row-sequentially in a single
pass, and for palette or sub-byte layouts, which cannot be subsampled by whole
pixels.

EXR: read one scanline at a time into a row buffer via a slice with a zero
yStride. Note Slice::Make treats a zero yStride as "compute the full row stride
for me", which sends row y past the end of a one row buffer, so the Slice
constructor is used with the base pointer offset by the data window origin. The
RGBA interface path (RY BY Y images) keeps its own chunked reader.

Measured on a 4096x4096 RGBF EXR: peak RSS 198 MB -> 18 MB, with pixel values
identical to the full decode at the corresponding coordinates.

